A||||2||MrPBody||Carbotech brake pads||||14||
Z||000000||MrPBody||02-03-2001||12:48 PM||||My '85 SE brakes have felt scary since I bought the car about 9 months ago.  They would stop OK in normal use but had little reserve stopping power.  In a panic stop, I could barely lock the front brakes.<P>About 350 miles ago, I installed a set of Carbotech Super Street front pads.  While I can offer only seat-of-the-pants evaluation (sorry, no decelerometer or braking distance figures) I can say that they work a hell of a lot better than my old pads.  They stop well from cold; they seem to respond progressively; and I can now lock the front brakes without too much grunt.<P>Installation was straightforward; the pads fit well.  I can recommend these as an alternative between carbon-metallic and ordinary parts-store pads.  At $109 per axle set plus shipping they are rather pricey, although cheaper than a Grand Am conversion.<P>Here's Carbotech's site:  <A HREF="http://www.carbotecheng.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.carbotecheng.com</A> <P>Has anyone else tried these?  What is your experience with them?<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000001||Wannabe||02-03-2001||04:24 PM||||Carbotech pads are outstanding. I used the Panther compound to bosst the braking power of my Talon TSi, which has ridicluously small brakes considering the car's power and weight, and they were fantastic. The Panther compound is a dual purpose street/track pad that's great for lapping days because you don't need to switch pads at the track. They do tend to squeel and dust a lot on the street though. They are also extremely gentle on the rotors considering the kind of heat they can handle. The best deal I've found for a street/performance pad is KVR. <A HREF="http://www.kvrperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.kvrperformance.com</A>   For track/street though, the Carbotech Panthers rule. Porterfield is also a good track/street pad, but about 1.5x the price of Carbotech.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||02-09-2001||10:16 PM||||OK, let me amend the above:  the new pads take a little more effort when stone cold.  But they're warm in a stop or two.<BR>||||reg||1
